# Next.js page structure

A `page.tsx` file exports **one** route component: the default `Page` (plus optional `metadata` / `generateMetadata` / route config). Do not define sibling components in the same file.

## Rules

- Put data fetching, `searchParams` / `params` awaiting, and JSX in the default `Page` itself.
- Prefer `export default async function Page(...)` when the route needs async work.
- Small Drizzle reads live in `Page`. Extract a query helper only when the query is large enough to justify it.
- Small route HTML lives in `Page`. Keep client forms and dialogs in `components/` — the page stays a server page. Extract a component when the page becomes hard to read.
- Do not extract page fetches into sibling files just to satisfy Suspense — keep the fetch in `Page`.
- Layout chrome, loading UI, and client wrappers stay in `layout.tsx`, `loading.tsx`, or `components/`.

## Examples

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — nested async component in the page file
async function PlayersGrid({ searchParams }: Props) {
  const data = await fetchRoster(searchParams);
  return <Grid players={data} />;
}

export default function Page({ searchParams }: Props) {
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<GridLoading />}>
      <PlayersGrid searchParams={searchParams} />
    </Suspense>
  );
}
```

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD — Drizzle read and small HTML in Page; client dialog stays extracted
export default async function Page() {
  const players = await db
    .select({ id: schema.clients.id, name: schema.clients.name })
    .from(schema.clients);

  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Players</h1>
      <CreatePlayerDialog />
      {players.length === 0 ? (
        <p>No players yet.</p>
      ) : (
        players.map((player) => <p key={player.id}>{player.name}</p>)
      )}
    </main>
  );
}
```
